[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

Take a moment, then walk me through how you'd approach this.

[candidate silent 12s]

[candidate · 1:42] Okay, so just to make sure I have it — revenue is actually up 15% over two years, but net margin fell from 24% to 6%. So this is really a cost problem, or possibly a mix problem where we're growing in low-margin places.

My approach would be: profit equals revenue minus costs, so first I'd decompose revenue into price times volume — are we growing because of more stores, more transactions per store, or higher prices? That matters, because 15% growth across 200 locations could actually be flat on a per-store basis.

Then costs: I'd split fixed versus variable. Variable would be coffee beans, milk, cups, hourly labor. Fixed would be rent, salaried staff, equipment, corporate overhead.

Then I'd compare the cost structure two years ago versus today as a percent of revenue, and find which line moved most.

Does that structure work for you? And do we know how many of those 200 locations are new?
