[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 8:11] Walk me through how you got the 23.4 for beans today. Check that against the cost figures you have, including the 37.5% on the other inputs.

[candidate · 9:41] Sure, let me redo it cleanly.

Base year, revenue 100, COGS 42. Beans 25% de 42, son 10.5. Other inputs = 42 menos 10.5, son 31.5.

Today, revenue 115, COGS 58% de 115, son 66.7. Other inputs grew 37.5%: 31.5 por 1.375 — un tercio de 31.5 son 10.5, más la mitad de eso, 1.5... me da 43.3. Beans = 66.7 menos 43.3, son 23.4. So the arithmetic holds.

But I should flag the assumption underneath it. I read the 37.5% as total dollars spent. If it actually means unit cost up 37.5%, then volume compounds on top: 31.5 por 1.375 por 1.15 son 49.8, and beans would be 66.7 menos 49.8, unos 16.9 — which lands almost exactly on the market-explained figure.

So the whole "unexplained bean overspend" conclusion hinges on that. Which reading is it?
