[interviewer · WRAP · 12:49] That's not in the information I have. If you were presenting to the CEO now, what's your recommendation?

[candidate · 14:19] My recommendation: Brew & Bean should raise menu prices, and that alone can restore most of the margin.

The root cause is clear. Input costs rose sharply — beans up 40%, other inputs up 37.5% — while menu prices have not moved in two years. Labor held at 22%, the footprint is unchanged at 200 stores, and revenue growth is pure volume. So the entire 18-point margin decline is absorbed input inflation.

Sizing it: COGS went from 42 to 58 points of revenue, entonces unos dieciséis puntos. To recover that at constant volume, I'd estimate a price increase in the mid-teens — call it 15% — would roughly close the gap.

Risks: specialty coffee demand may be price-elastic, and we don't have competitor pricing, so a blanket 15% could cost traffic.

So I'd phase it — pilot 8–10% in a subset of markets, pair it with high-margin food and add-on mix, and hedge beans — then measure volume response before rolling out chain-wide.
